'''Kaleigh Trace''' (born 1986) is a Canadian writer, sex educator, and therapist based in [[Toronto]], Ontario known for her candid discussions of sexuality and disability. Her 2014 memoir ''Hot, Wet, and Shaking: How I Learned to Talk About Sex'', which chronicles her experiences as a disabled, [[queer]], feminist sex educator, won the 2015 [[Evelyn Richardson Award|Evelyn Richardson Memorial Non-Fiction Award]] at the [[Atlantic Book Awards]]. Trace republished an updated edition of the memoir in 2024.

==Biography==
Trace was born in 1986. She and her family experienced a car accident in 1995, which left her with a severe injury to her [[spinal cord]]. As a result of the accident, she spent part of her childhood in a wheelchair.{{Sfnmp|1a1=Trace|1y=2014|1pp=2-3|2a1=Fowles|2y=2014}} She graduated from [[Dalhousie University]] with a [[Bachelor of Arts]] in 2009,{{Sfnmp|Trace|2024}} and subsequently became employed at [[Venus Envy (sex shop)|Venus Envy]], a Canadian [[sex shop]] and book retailer, where her work included [[sex education]]. Trace credited her time at Venus Envy with expanding her knowledge on human sexuality.{{Sfnmp|Moran|2014|CBC Radio|2018}} While working at Venus Envy, Trace began writing a blog, ''The Fucking Facts'', in which she discussed deeply personal topics such as an [[abortion]] she had two years prior to starting the blog.{{Sfnmp|Moran|2014}} Trace's blog would serve as inspiration her book ''Hot, Wet, and Shaking''.{{Sfnmp|Moran|2014|Nauss|2014}} In 2013, Trace received internet attention for her parody song of [[Blurred Lines]] by [[Robin Thicke]] entitled ''Ask First''. The song was co-written by Trace and other Venus Envy staff members.{{Sfnmp|Cruikshank|2013|CTV Atlantic|2013}}

Trace's book ''Hot, Wet, and Shaking'' was released in 2014.{{Sfnmp|Trace|2024}} The memoir details her experience as a disabled, queer, feminist sex educator, exploring her personal story with sexuality through the lens of her spinal cord injury and work at Venus Envy. Through honest and humorous anecdotes, Trace challenges conventional narratives of sex, covering topics from struggles with [[orgasm]] and comical mishaps to serious issues like abortion and [[ableism]].{{Sfnmp|National Post|2014|Willard|2014|Nauss|2014}} The book was the winner of the [[Evelyn Richardson Award|Evelyn Richardson Memorial Non-Fiction Award]] at the [[Atlantic Book Awards]] in 2015.{{Sfnmp|Robertson|2015|Writers' Federation of Nova Scotia|2015|Smith|2024}}

Trace remained employed at Venus Envy until moving to [[Toronto]] in 2017. She commuted to study at the [[University of Guelph]], where she received her [[Master of Science]] in couples and family therapy. She worked at a Toronto therapy clinic, before eventually opening a private practice.{{Sfnmp|Trace|2024}}

In October 2022, Trace was diagnosed with terminal [[bladder cancer]]. The same year, she was contacted by Invisible Publishing regarding the republication of a 10-year anniversary edition of ''Hot, Wet, and Shaking''. Although she stated she was now "much more reluctant" to openly discuss her sex life, she agreed to the republication, calling the book a "much-needed celebration of sex".{{Sfnmp|Trace|2024}} The republished book included a new introduction, as well as chapters reflecting her cancer diagnosis.{{Sfnmp|Gamage|2024}} For the launch of the 10-year anniversary edition, Trace hosted a performance event including standup comedy, games, and reading of short stories. The success of the event led to her hosting another performance called Little Deaths Salon in Toronto with [[Christa Couture]] in July 2025,{{Sfnmp|Smith|2025}} returning on 17 October 2025 at the [[Buddies in Bad Times Theatre]].{{Sfnp|NOW Toronto|2025}}

==''Hot, Wet, and Shaking''==
Trace's memoir, ''Hot, Wet, and Shaking: How I Learned to Talk About Sex'', was first published on 17 September 2014 by Invisible Publishing of [[Halifax, Nova Scotia|Halifax]], Nova Scotia.{{Sfnmp|Willard|2014}} The book draws from Trace's experiences as a disabled, queer, feminist sex educator at [[Venus Envy (sex shop)|Venus Envy]], and was inspired by her blog, ''The Fucking Facts''.{{Sfnmp|National Post|2014|Moran|2014}} The book explores her personal story with sexuality, shaped by a spinal cord injury from a car accident in 1995, through candid and humorous anecdotes.{{Sfnmp|Willard|2014|Nauss|2014}} It addresses topics such as struggles with orgasm, abortion, [[ableism]], and the complexities of sexual identity, challenging conventional narratives surrounding sex and disability.{{Sfnmp|National Post|2014|Willard|2014|Nauss|2014}} The book was praised for its balance of humour and emotion, noting Trace's ability to seamlessly transition between recounting comical mishaps and reflecting on serious topics.{{Sfnmp|National Post|2014|Rayner|2015}}

In 2014, ''Hot, Wet, and Shaking'' was named the gold winner for best book by ''[[The Coast (newspaper)|The Coast]]'', a Halifax newspaper.{{Sfnmp|The Coast|2014}} The following year, the book was the winner of the [[Evelyn Richardson Award|Evelyn Richardson Memorial Non-Fiction Award]] at the 2015 [[Atlantic Book Awards]].{{Sfnmp|Robertson|2015|Writers' Federation of Nova Scotia|2015|Smith|2024}} In 2017, it was listed among 150 Books of Influence by the Nova Scotia Library Association.{{Sfnmp|1a1=Emery|1a2=Lelliott|1y=2017}}

A German translation of the book was released in 2020, published by {{ill|Orlanda Verlag|de}} of [[Berlin]]. The book was translated by [[Penelope Dützmann]], a linguistics professor at the [[University of Cologne]]. It was presented at the 2021 [[Frankfurt Book Fair]] as part of Canada's Guest of Honor appearance in collaboration with the [[Canada House, Berlin|Canadian Embassy in Germany]].{{Sfnmp|Dützmann|2021}} 

A 10th anniversary edition of the book was published in 2024, also by Invisible Publishing. The revised edition includes a new introduction, as well as new chapters reflecting on Trace's cancer diagnosis two years prior to the republication, offering further insight into her evolving perspective on the topics of sex and mortality.{{Sfnmp|Gamage|2024|Trace|2024}} Despite her initial reluctance to revisit the topics discussed in the book, Trace described it as a "much-needed celebration of sex".{{Sfnmp|Trace|2024}} The same year, the book was listed on ''The Hamilton Review of Books''{{'}} bestsellers list for July 2024.{{Sfnmp|Allen|2024}}

